\subsection{How large must $k$ be?}
Figure~\ref{fig:main} gives the main result. Running a handful of benchmarks removes most of the error of the column-mean predictor: with three benchmarks the factor model with greedy selection closes 0.81 (MTEB-Eng), 0.82 (MTEB-Multi) and 0.96 (HELM MMLU) of the gap between the column mean and LOBO (standardised RMSE 0.62, 0.59, 0.53, against $1.04$, $1.03$, $1.16$ for the column mean and $0.47$, $0.43$, $0.49$ for LOBO). Beyond that the pools differ (Table~\ref{tab:kstar}). On HELM MMLU one benchmark closes 0.90 ($k^*(0.9)=1$, interval $1$--$2$) and $k^*(0.95)=3$: the matrix is dominated by one general factor, and the H1 outcome is ``dominated by a general factor''. On the MTEB pools the curve is slow in its tail: the headline $k^*(0.9)$ is 9 on MTEB-Eng and 30, the grid maximum, on MTEB-Multi, with intervals $[3,>30]$ and $[8,>30]$ and 3\% and 26\% of bootstrap replicates censored; the factor model alone needs 20 and more than 30, and 0.95 is not reached on either pool. Both MTEB outcomes are ``above window'', so H1 (``knee near $r_{\rm eff}$'') is supported on none of the three pools. Neither $k^*$ is stable: it depends on the level, the predictor, and, on MTEB-Multi, on the transform (the factor model alone reaches 0.9 at $k=15$ with the identity transform and not within 30 with the logit; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:robust}), and under the time-forward split it is 30 on MTEB-Eng; we therefore report $k^*$ with its interval and censoring, not as a point value.

