\subsection{Which benchmarks to run?}
Greedy A-optimal selection is better than random selection on all three pools (Figure~\ref{fig:gap}), by a grid-averaged paired gap in RMSE of $-0.038$ [$-0.058,-0.013$] on MTEB-Eng, $-0.076$ [$-0.085,-0.066$] on MTEB-Multi and $-0.044$ [$-0.065,-0.026$] on HELM MMLU, i.e. 6.1, 11.3 and 7.6\% of the random error. Only MTEB-Multi meets the pre-specified margin of 10\%, so H2 holds there and the others are a clear but small improvement. The benefit is concentrated at small $k$: 5--18\% of the random error at $k\le3$ (the interval includes zero on MTEB-Eng at $k=3$), 1--5\% at $k=10$, below 3\% at $k=20$. Greedy D-optimality and $k$-medoids on benchmark correlations are as good as greedy A; maximum-variance selection is no better than random, and leverage scores and pivoted QR are \emph{worse} than random on the MTEB pools (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:grid}). Adaptive selection gains at most 0.014 RMSE over greedy A (MTEB-Multi only, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:adaptive}). The factor model is no more accurate than simple predictors on the same sets: ridge, $k$-NN and Ledoit--Wolf conditioning have equal or lower RMSE on the MTEB pools (by up to 0.026) and ridge and Ledoit--Wolf are 0.011--0.013 lower on HELM MMLU (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:predictors}), so the factor model buys no accuracy over simple predictors, the selector's gain is modest and shared by simpler selectors, and the risk formula gives the curve's shape only. Under the transform not chosen on the training folds the gaps are $-0.017$ [$-0.051,0.037$] on MTEB-Eng (2.6\%), $-0.075$ on MTEB-Multi (11.4\%) and $-0.050$ on HELM MMLU (8.4\%), so the MTEB-Eng result depends on the transform and the MTEB-Multi margin does not (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:robust}).

