\subsection{New families and newer models}
Holding out whole families costs little on the MTEB pools and more on HELM MMLU (Figure~\ref{fig:shift}): at $k=3$ the RMSE of a family-held-out model is 2\% above that of a random-split model on both MTEB pools (0.616 against 0.604; 0.590 against 0.578; the paired difference has an interval including zero on MTEB-Eng and just excluding it on MTEB-Multi) and 14\% above on HELM MMLU (0.534 against 0.467), where 21 families share 79 models, so holding out a family removes a larger part of the history. Predicting models released \emph{later} is harder: on the same test models the RMSE at $k=3$ is 9\% higher than under the grouped split on MTEB-Eng (interval includes zero; 10\% over the grid, interval excludes zero) and 18\% higher on MTEB-Multi (14\% over the grid). The advantage of greedy selection over random does not shrink under shift (gaps $-0.041$ and $-0.086$ on the time-forward split, against $-0.038$ and $-0.076$ grouped; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:robust}). Errors are biased against the strongest models: at $k=3$ the strongest quintile of MTEB-Eng models is predicted too low by 0.10 [$0.02$, $0.19$] standard deviations under the grouped split and 0.31 [$0.16$, $0.47$] under the time-forward split (MTEB-Multi: 0.09 and 0.16), and the weakest HELM MMLU models are predicted too high by 0.14 [$0.00$, $0.24$]. Part of this is an artefact, since quintiles formed on the mean score, which contains the targets, show bias even for a correct model (0.04 in simulation); stratifying on the observed scores leaves 0.06 [$-0.02$, $0.15$] on MTEB-Eng, 0.07 [$0.03$, $0.11$] on MTEB-Multi and none on HELM MMLU, so we do not claim a general bias against strong models; the time-forward values are not decomposed.

