\subsection{Does the closed-form risk track the curve?}
With the true parameters on synthetic data the formula~\eqref{eq:risk} is exact up to Monte Carlo error (median relative gap of the reducible part 0.5\%, maximum 1.5\%; Figure~\ref{fig:theory}, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:theory}). With parameters fitted to $M=60$ to 2\,000 history models the median gap is 26.7\% to 1.5\%, falling roughly like $1/M$, which is the estimation noise of $W$ and $D$ that the formula ignores; the median meets the 10\% tolerance from $M=250$. On the real data (MTEB-Multi and HELM MMLU in the logit-transformed space) the median gap is 36\% (MTEB-Multi), 47\% (MTEB-Eng) and 69\% (HELM MMLU): H4 fails, and the pre-specified tolerance of 15\% is far from met. Most of the gap is a near-constant offset of about 0.08 in squared standardised units, whose cause we have not identified; without it the median gap in the $k$-dependent part is 8.5\% (MTEB-Eng), 3.2\% (MTEB-Multi) and 23\% (HELM MMLU), so the formula follows the shape of the curve on the MTEB pools and not on HELM MMLU.

Appendix Table~\ref{tab:coherence} asks whether low coherence explains why random selection is close to greedy; it does not: the coherence of the fitted loadings is about 3--4 on all three pools, near the low end of our synthetic range of 3--10, yet greedy A removes 35--61\% of the reducible risk of random selection at $k=1,3,10$ and on the MTEB pools the relative gap does not shrink up to $k=10$ (the absolute gap does); across synthetic coherence levels the gap does not vary monotonically (at $k=3$: 51, 53, 66 and 50\%). The small RMSE gap (6--11\%) is probably because the reducible part is a small share of the error. The sampled submodularity ratio is 0.97--0.99 on the pools (median over fits; individual fits 0.89--1.00), an upper bound on the true ratio, so we claim no greedy guarantee.

\subsection{A leaderboard with missing cells}\label{sec:sparse}
The leaderboard an evaluator holds has missing cells, so we repeat the protocol on MTEB matrices with their real gaps: the English tasks reported for at least 20 models (445 models $\times$ 335 tasks, 21\% of cells observed) and English and all-task subsets trimmed to 30, 50 and 70\% observed (Table~\ref{tab:sparse}; Appendix~\ref{app:sparse}). A test model has at least 30 observed cells and is held out by family; its observed cells are split at random into 30\% targets and 70\% candidates, among which $k$ are chosen. Predictors are the factor model fitted by masked EM and the pairwise-complete correlation matrix shrunk toward the identity (``pw''); the LOBO reference conditions on all candidates with the better of the two, so closure can exceed 1.
Greedy A beats random selection at every $k=1,\dots,20$ on every subset for both predictors (the interval excludes zero in all cells but pw at $k=20$ on the 21\% subset). At $k=3$ the gap is 5--7\% of the random error with pw and 6--24\% with the factor model. Greedy A closes 0.71--0.80 of the gap to LOBO at $k=3$ (pw), random selection 0.55--0.74; greedy A reaches 0.9 at $k=6$ to 20, with wide intervals, and random selection later or not within 20. These runs are exploratory (the 10\% margin was fixed for the dense test, and the subsets were trimmed greedily to a density), and the results do not say why greedy selection wins. Their RMSE is not comparable with the dense pools, which remain the controlled experiment.
